-- title:
--   Normalizing a color class and constructing its retained prime data
-- statement:
--   Write $S,\Omega,\Delta$ for machine space, word magnitude, and depth limits. Let $n>0$ and $x:\{0,\ldots,n-1\}\to\mathbb Z$ satisfy $|x_i|\le U$. Fix a coloring parameter of width $\operatorname{inputBits}(n)+1$. Memory stores the input array, exact 19-word parameter record, and six coloring fields, all ending before free address $f$.
--
--   Assume $\operatorname{radix}(n)\ge4$, the input-host space and word bounds, $S\le\Omega$, and the specified input-host depth bound. The routine returns
--   $$r=\operatorname{prime}(f,n,\operatorname{bandSize}(n)),$$
--   the address of the resulting prime-data record. It retains strictly increasing normalized lists for color classes $0,1,2$, with the third class's values negated, together with their band structures, canonical owner records, raw-$A$ incidence count, prime pool, and full common-modulus chain. The normalized header and prime-data record satisfy the exact combined output predicate. Every cell below $f$ is preserved, within the stated input-host time budget.
--
--   This prepares the normalized sources and modulus data for one encoded coloring.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/c0ldzy17/three-sum-lean/blob/2b639655e07421b9ac743de9090459bab473a7c0/Submission/ThreeSum/LightEncodedColorPrimeHost.lean#L61-L158

